Multiple data recession Taiwan's economy performs poorly in the beginning of the year
Xinhua News Agency Taipei, February 11 (Xinhua News Agency) (Xinhua News Agency) Export trade volume rose five times, the Manufacturing Procurement Manager Index (PMI) contracted for seven consecutive months, the Consumer Price Index (CPI) fell three times... Recently, Taiwanese authorities continued to release economic data for January 2023.
According to analysts, Taiwan's economic performance at the beginning of the year was poor and growth pressures were high.
According to Taiwan's Financial Affairs Directorate, Taiwan exported $31.5 billion in January this year, a decrease of 22.2% year on year, a fifth consecutive month of negative growth.
The volume of exports of 11 major export categories decreased compared to the same period last year.
Plastics and their products, as well as optical instruments, fell the most, falling by 37.6% and 35.7% respectively.
Exports of electronic parts decreased by 20.1% per year, the largest decrease in 11 years.
Of these, integrated circuits declined by 18.3% annually, the largest decline in 14 years.
The department's head of statistics, Domina, said that this shows that the weakness of consumer electronics demand is spreading, and the electronics industry is entering a period of correction.
Statistics show that Taiwan's imports and exports doubled in January, surpassing just US$2.34 billion, reaching their lowest level in nearly three years.
The Taiwanese government's financial affairs authority believes that the global demand for terminals is declining, industry adjustments to inventory suppress the willingness of suppliers to buy, the export momentum is unlikely to improve in the short term, and Taiwan is facing a recession in exports in the first quarter of this year that is higher than expected.
According to the Institute of Chinese Economics in Taiwan, the Taiwan Manufacturing Procurement Managers' Index (PMI) in January this year was 40.4% after a seasonal adjustment, down 3.3 percentage points from last December, and contracted for seven consecutive months.
In the system of economic monitoring indicators, the PMI is 50 per cent declining and the index is greater than 50 per cent, representing economic expansion and contraction.
Among the five indicators comprising the January Taiwan PMI, new orders, production and manpower contraction, declining supplier delivery times, and inventory contraction.
The six largest industries reported PMI contraction, with each industry ranked according to the contraction rate as electrical and silicon machinery industry (35.1%), electronics and optics industry (35.2%), chemical and biomedical industry (39.2%), basic raw materials industry (42.4%), transportation industry (42.6%) and food and textile industry (45.7%).
In terms of prices, the Taiwanese government's Office of the Chief Accountant announced that Taiwan's annual CPI growth rate was 3.04 percent in January this year, breaking another 3 percent since July last year.
Among them, vegetable prices have skyrocketed by 28.25%, reaching a new 17-month high.
Chairman of Taipei's General Directorate, Liu Xiaobo, said that the increase in food prices did exceed expectations, excluding Spring Festival factors, and that the average CPI for January and February should be close to 2.71% in December last year, with inflation pressure still in place and still high.
Experts have expressed pessimism about the outlook for Taiwan's economy this year, as reported by the inland media such as the Comprehensive Taiwan Joint Report, the Yahoo Financial Report, and others.
Professor Liu Daeng, of the Department of Economics at Taipei Central University, analyzed that a recession in exports could cause cash flow problems for export manufacturers and an increase in the risk of debt default.
At the same time, decreased orders have led manufacturers to cut labor costs, affecting the labor market and household economy, and in turn impacting consumption capacity.
A researcher at the Taiwan Economic Research Institute (Taiwan Institute of Economics) said inflation in Europe and the United States is unresolved this year, demand continues to shrink, and Taiwan's export recession is expected to continue into the first half of the year.
As Taiwan's economy relies on exports and is affected by the export slump, economic growth throughout the year will not be as strong as last year.
